    {
        "content": "<p>I want a <code>conjecture</code> keyword that acts similarly to the <code>theorem</code> keyword, allowing a <code>Prop</code> to be stated and providing an opportunity to attempt to prove the <code>Prop</code>. However, a <code>conjecture</code> must not be allowed in proofs of anything other than <code>conjecture</code>s.</p>\n<p>Ideally, when compiling a <code>conjecture</code> proved without relying on any non-proved <code>conjecture</code>, a warning/message to use the <code>theorem</code> keyword should be visible.<br>\nIdeally, <code>#print axioms SomeConjecture</code> should include [any <code>conjecture</code>s that SomeConjecture relies on] that use a sorry.</p>\n<p>I did not find a way to do this but would not be surprised if someone already implemented something similar.<br>\nThanks in advance.</p>",
        "id": 579824002,
        "sender_full_name": "Mark Andrew Gerads",
        "timestamp": 1773707668
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>we have <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=proof_wanted#doc\">docs#proof_wanted</a> is in <code>Batteries.Util.ProofWanted</code>, is this similar?</p>",
        "id": 579824086,
        "sender_full_name": "Aaron Liu",
        "timestamp": 1773707716
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I'll see. Thanks.</p>",
        "id": 579824142,
        "sender_full_name": "Mark Andrew Gerads",
        "timestamp": 1773707756
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Note the <code>proof_wanted</code> is like <code>example</code>: it leaves no trace in the environment, so in particular you can not in any way build on a <code>proof_wanted</code> statement in later declarations.</p>",
        "id": 579824380,
        "sender_full_name": "Kim Morrison",
        "timestamp": 1773707874
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>It is very close.<br>\nIf I have this:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">proof_wanted</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">stuff</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">k0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k3</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">hyperoperation</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">k1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">hyperoperation</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">k1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">k2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k3</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>then I could definitely attempt the proof with a <code>theorem</code> keyword, perhaps in another file (in some folder that <code>lake exe mk_all</code> is not able to find) to avoid accidentally committing an incomplete theorem. I want to be able to do the following:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">proof_wanted</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">stuff</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">k0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k3</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">hyperoperation</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">k1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">hyperoperation</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">k1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">k2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">k3</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">grind</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">simp</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">try?</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"gr\">sorry</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Having <code>proof_wanted</code> partial proofs that are treated as <code>theorem</code>s invisible to every other piece of Lean code (except the proof checker) would be nice and be worth contributing where <code>proof_wanted</code> is normally used.<br>\nI suppose implementing something like 1 <code>proof_wanted</code> refencing another is not nearly as important to me as being able to use <code>:=</code> with <code>proof_wanted</code>.</p>",
        "id": 579826155,
        "sender_full_name": "Mark Andrew Gerads",
        "timestamp": 1773709125
    }
]